 ## Background
-The Cut-Border Machine (CBM) was originally proposed for triangle meshes by Gumhold et al. [Gumhold et al. 1999] and extended to tetrahedral meshes in the context of segment-based tetrahedral meshing and rendering [S1]. The work by Yoon et al. [2005] highlighted the importance of mesh layout for rendering performance, which motivated the extension of CBM to support multi-resolution meshes and cross-segment incidencies while respecting pre-computed layouts [S1].
+The Cut-Border Machine (CBM) was extended to tetrahedral meshes in the context of segment-based tetrahedral meshing and rendering [S1], building on data structures originally proposed for triangle meshes [Gumhold et al. 1999]. The work by Yoon et al. [2005] highlighted the importance of mesh layout for rendering performance, which motivated the extension of CBM to support multi-resolution meshes and cross-segment incidencies while respecting pre-computed layouts [S1].
 
